---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CtaRow from "../../components/CtaRow.astro";
import { linkClass } from "../../lib/links";
import { withBase } from "../../lib/base";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Catalog sales trends, Q1 2025 | FFL Accelerator"
  description="Q1 2025 catalog conversation: cooler unit demand after the 2020 to 2023 surge, carry and training SKUs still working, larger kits softer."
  path="/trends/2025-q1/"
  noindex
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-12 sm:py-14">
    <p
      class="mb-3 text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-gold"
    >
      Trends · Q1 2025
    </p>
    <h1 class="mb-4 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white">
      Catalog sales trends in Q1 2025
    </h1>
    <p class="mb-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-300">
      The first quarter of 2025 felt like a continuation of the post-surge
      hangover: fewer panic tickets, more shopping around, and a floor that
      still moved carry models and low-cost practice supplies.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">What the quarter felt like</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-400">
      Dealers we talk to described a selective buyer: compact carry models and
      the holster, optic, and magazine ecosystem around them, plus rimfire for
      range time, while larger kits and bulk cases sat longer. Mid-tier
      compacts still did the work; premium collectible tickets were quieter.
      Show-week gadgets showed up in conversation more than they showed up as
      volume.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-white">Categories that still worked</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm text-zinc-400">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Carry compacts.</strong> Micro and compact
        models remained the default first answer for walk-ins. Aftermarket grip
        modules, optic plates, and holsters rode along.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Rimfire trainers.</strong> When centerfire
        practice felt expensive, a trainer and a brick of rimfire were the
        honest bundle.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Small accessories, not another long kit.</strong>
        Slides, threaded barrels, lasers, and ear pro are where a slow unit
        quarter can still print margin, if the site and the counter can find
        them.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-white">Categories that sat</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm text-zinc-400">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Familiar platforms.</strong> After years of
        surge buying, first-time demand was not the story. Discounting a stale
        wall is merchandising, not a national series.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Pump models.</strong> Home-defense
        shoppers kept asking about softer-shooting semis. Pumps did not vanish;
        they stopped being automatic.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Bulk cases.</strong> Fewer closet-fill
        tickets. More small buys for this weekend.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-white">
      If you mention 2025 units, say which number
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-400">
      NSSF, citing Gearfire’s 2025 Annual Industry Report (23 Feb 2026), put
      same-store new units at about <strong class="text-zinc-200"
        >−13% vs 2024</strong
      >.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-10 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-300">
      If the floor is moving and the website is not, that is the FFL
      Accelerator job. $569 a month. You own the site.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6">
      <CtaRow />
    </div>

    <p class="mt-10 text-sm text-zinc-500">
      Next:{" "}
      <a href={withBase("trends/2025-q2")} class={linkClass}>Q2 2025 →</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
